      "content": "## THE ALL-STAR CENSUS: DREW OWNS THIRTEEN OF THEM, DAVID OWNS THE WRONG HALF OF OHTANI, AND THE AL'S STARTING SECOND BASEMAN IS NAMED ERNIE\n\nThe All-Star Game is Tuesday at Citizens Bank Park, and Ernie spent the weekend doing what Ernie does: counting. This census is expansive by design: every player *named* to the AL or NL rosters counts \u2014 starters, reserves, replacements, the injured and the declining alike. Esteban's Munetaka Murakami is on the shelf and still counts. Ernie honors the naming, not the availability. The result is a census with a runaway leader. Drew owns **thirteen All-Stars**, which is more than most actual MLB franchises placed, and the roll call is the punchline: alongside the Witts and Sotos sit Bazzana, McGonigle, Messick, and the rest of the prospect stockpile Drew has been hoarding like canned goods since February. The farm system finally produced a literal All-Star haul, five deep.\n\nThe best story is the split custody. Yahoo carries Shohei Ohtani as two roster entries, and the league divided him accordingly: Andrew owns Ohtani the Batter, who was voted the NL's starting DH (he'll skip the game with knee discomfort, but the honor stands). David owns Ohtani the Pitcher, who was not selected. David owns the wrong half of the best player alive \u2014 this on a team named for Cal Raleigh, who also didn't make it. Two All-Stars and a non-selected hemisphere of a deity: last place in the census.\n\nElsewhere in the count: Greg owns Ernie Clement, the American League's *starting second baseman*, and your narrator is contractually delighted \u2014 even Greg's organic-intelligence purism must concede that an Ernie cracked a big-league starting lineup this week. Esteban's Dillon Dingler made the AL roster, meaning the Dinglerberries feature an actual All-Star Dingler, nominative determinism finally settling its tab. Reid's Vladimir Guerrero Jr. was voted the AL's starting first baseman and then declined with back trouble \u2014 even Reid's good news arrives with an asterisk stapled to it. And your commissioner claims five, though Aaron Judge won't play, which means Ryan's headline All-Star contribution is Logan Webb looking defensible at $33 for exactly one week in July.\n\n| Manager | Team | All-Stars | Headliner |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Drew | Move the HR Derby to Las Vegas | 13 | Bobby Witt Jr. |\n| Esteban | Dinglerberries | 9 | Shea Langeliers (AL starting C) |\n| Casey | 'Tungsten Arm' O'Doyle | 8 | Kyle Schwarber |\n| Greg | Lollygaggers | 7 | Ernie Clement (AL starting 2B) |\n| Christopher | Chris's Team | 7 | Bryce Harper, in his home park |\n| Andrew | The LA Salary Caps | 7 | Shohei Ohtani (the batting half) |\n| Jimmy Bowman | Le Bon Mart\u00e9 | 5 | Mike Trout |\n| Ryan L | Sandberg's Cubbies | 5 | Aaron Judge (injured, absent) |\n| Alex | Fire Craig Counsell | 5 | Paul Skenes |\n| Reid | The Sin Eaters! | 4 | Vladimir Guerrero Jr. (declined) |\n| Matthew Kelly | The Parte's Over | 3 | Dylan Cease |\n| David | Raleighing the Loser's Bracket | 2 | Michael Wacha |\n\nOne footnote for the census purists: expansive as this count is, it stops at the water's edge of Justin Verlander, named to the American League roster at 43 as the commissioner's legacy selection \u2014 an All-Star the way a retired number is a player. He is owned by nobody in this league, and for once Ernie cannot fault the twelve of you. Being named an All-Star is an honor. Rostering one is a decision.\n"
